/-!
# Zero cycles and closed points with separable residue field

Let `k` be a field and let `X` be a smooth projective scheme over `k`. Stacks 0FH5 says that
`CH_0(X) = A_0(X)` is generated by the classes of the closed points of `X` whose residue fields
are separable over `k`. This file states that assertion as membership in the subgroup generated
by those classes, reduces it to the per-point statement produced by the Bertini argument of the
source proof, and proves it outright when `k` is perfect.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Chow.HasSeparableResidueField` — the residue field at a point of a scheme over `k` is
  separable over `k`.
* `Stacks.Chow.separablePointClasses` — the set of classes in `A_0(X)` of the closed points of
  `X` with separable residue field, the generating set of Stacks 0FH5.
* `Stacks.Chow.SeparablePointComplementaryCycles` — the output of the Bertini step of the proof
  of Stacks 0FH5: for every closed point `x` there are zero cycles `Z`, `Z'` supported on closed
  points with separable residue fields such that `[x] + [Z] = [Z']` in `A_0(X)`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Chow.mem_closure_separablePointClasses` — the passage from one closed point to all of
  `A_0(X)`: given `Stacks.Chow.SeparablePointComplementaryCycles k`, every class in `A_0(X)` lies
  in the subgroup generated by the classes of the closed points with separable residue field.
  That input is proved for every field in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ZeroCyclesSeparablePointsBertini`, which imports this file
  and states Stacks 0FH5 unconditionally as
  `Stacks.Chow.mem_closure_separablePointClasses_of_isProjectiveMorphism`.
* `Stacks.Chow.separablePointComplementaryCycles_of_perfectField` and
  `Stacks.Chow.mem_closure_separablePointClasses_of_perfectField` — the special case of a perfect
  field, where every closed point already has separable residue field. This is the first
  reduction of the source proof, which disposes of characteristic zero and of perfect fields
  (finite fields among them) before assuming `k` infinite of characteristic `p > 0`.
* `Stacks.Chow.isClosed_singleton_of_relativeDimension_eq_zero` — a point of delta-dimension zero
  of a scheme locally of finite type over a field is closed.
* `Stacks.Chow.isAlgebraic_residueField_of_isClosed` — the residue field at a closed point of a
  scheme locally of finite type over `k` is algebraic over `k`.
* `Stacks.Chow.mk_mem_closure_separablePointClasses` — a zero cycle supported on closed points
  with separable residue fields has class in that subgroup.

## Implementation notes

The base is `Stacks.Chow.fieldChowSituation k`, whose dimension function vanishes, so the
delta-dimension of an integral closed subscheme of `X` is its dimension over `k` and
`Stacks.Chow.chowGroup (fieldChowSituation k) p 0` is `CH_0(X)`. The class of a closed point `x`
is the class of the fundamental cycle of `Stacks.Chow.integralComponentAt x`, the reduced induced
structure on the closure of `x`; that is the spelling of a prime cycle used throughout chapter
42 here. "Generated by" is `AddSubgroup.closure` membership, as in
`Stacks.Chow.ChowClassesGeneratedByBaseFlatPullbacks`: Chow groups are bare abelian groups, so
there is no `Submodule.span`, and membership avoids having to name the ambient `⊤`.

Smoothness and projectivity are stated with `AlgebraicGeometry.Smooth` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsProjectiveMorphism`, clause (1) of Definition 01W8 in its H-projective form;
properness is a separate instance binder because the Chow group of `p` needs
`LocallyOfFiniteType p` at elaboration time and `IsProjectiveMorphism` is a predicate, not a
class. No hypothesis on the characteristic or the cardinality of `k` is imposed.

### The per-point statement

`Stacks.Chow.SeparablePointComplementaryCycles` is the output of Steps 3 to 8 of the source
proof. Those steps fix a closed point `x`, pass from `k` to the finite separable extension
`k' = H^0(X, O_X)` so that `X` becomes geometrically integral (Stacks 056T, 0BUG, 0FD1), choose a
power of an ample sheaf which is very ample and whose sections vanishing at `x` generate it away
from `x` and surject onto `m_x L_x / m_x^2 L_x` (Stacks 01VU, 0FD5, 01Q3), cut `X` by `d` such
sections so that `H_1 ∩ ... ∩ H_d = x ⊔ Z` scheme-theoretically, make `Z` étale over `k` by
applying Bertini `d` times (Stacks 0FD6), and compare with a second general complete intersection
`Z'` (Stacks 0FFD) to reach `[x] ~ -[Z] + [Z']`. The statement is that last line, with "étale
over `k`" replaced by its consequence for the supports of the two cycles. Those steps are done
in `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ZeroCyclesSeparableReduction` and
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.ZeroCyclesSeparablePointsBertini` (as
`Stacks.Chow.separablePointComplementaryCycles`, for every field); they import this file, which is
why the statement is a definition here and an argument of `mem_closure_separablePointClasses`.
What is proved in this file is the passage from the single point `x` to all of `A_0(X)`: every
class is the class of a cycle with finite support, every point of that support is closed, and the
subgroup generated by the separable point classes absorbs the finite sums and the multiplicities.

